WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.532
When reading a CTE query, do not read top to bottom as one giant statement.

00:00:04.532 --> 00:00:07.227
First understand the CTE by itself.

00:00:07.227 --> 00:00:10.357
Then ask what the final query does with that stage.

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:12.917
Here the CTE filters patients.

00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:15.922
The final query joins admissions and aggregates.

00:00:15.922 --> 00:00:20.347
This staged reading style is one reason CTEs improve clarity.

00:00:20.347 --> 00:00:26.367
A CTE gives a name to an intermediate result so a complex query can be read in stages.

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:33.337
Use it to clarify grain changes and major transformations, not simply to break every two lines into a new

00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:39.920
block. A useful CTE name describes what the rows represent, such as patient_totals or ranked_visits.
